A short introduction:

Although what is being said below is a all-India phenomenon, I have tried to 
explain the point using Talcher as an example.

Everybody of us is quite aware  that  Orissa has huge reserves of coal in the 
Angul-Talcher region, yet it is hardly useful because of its very
high ash contents. The ash content of Talcher coal is well above 33%, while a
safe limit is well below 21%. Therefore the coal in its present impurity 
can't be used for industrial production, unless otherwise special technique
is employed to purify the coal to the acceptance level (one of my senior 
friends is currently looking for similar opportunity). The large integrated 
steel plants of India, the major consumers of coal, are therefore meeting 
their coal demands elsewhere. I am highlighting this point as someone among us  
might think of starting a kiln for purifying Talcher coal and then sell it
at a lucrative price to the industrial giants. Hardly anybody has given any
serious thought on this line. The one to lead is going to gain the most.

So, Orissa is left with only one option. To use this huge reserve in the 
boilers of Thermal power plants. But with this originates the mammoth problem 
Dr. Powell and his team  helping solve. Thermal Power plant uses coal to 
heat the boilers which in turn produces steam and blast used for power and
steel production. If the coal is properly grounded using special technique
(which most power plants use these days) the high ash content coal can be 
rendered useful. But at a huge price. Over a period of last 20 to 30 years
of unrestraint use the Talcher region has accumulated huge reserves of 
untreated fly-ash, as a by product of burning high ash content coal. Untreated
fly-ash is highly toxic and environmentally damaging. Adversly affected is the
drinking water of the surrounding region.

At all India level the problem becomes alarming if we consider the rate at which
thermal power plants are mushrooming to meet our increasing development needs.
Keeping this problem in mind, Government of India organised several conferences
and meetings. The one viable solution that came out of these deliberations is
to use this huge fly-ash reserve for road construction.
It is at this point Dr. Powell and his team deferred with this general view.
The team were of the opinion that even if the road construction took an 
alarming growth it would not be possible to use up even the fraction of the old reserves. A different solution was needed.
Over a period of continuos research Dr. Powell's team came up with a revolution-
ary solution.

This solution is the subject matter of this discussion. The team argued that
the fly-ash could be effectively use to restore the agriculture lands of
trace elements which have been depleting because of repeated use over long
period. The fly-ash is highly toxic which is harmful for human existance in 
the surrounding area. But fly-ash contains high percentage of trace elements,
which if fixed would render fly-ash non toxic, while improving the quality of
the farming land.
The idea was to initiate short term feasibility study in the affected area.
Kurunti village, near Talcher-Angul and a region outside the Bhubaneswar city
have been chosen for the feasibility study.

The Experimental mixture:

The idea was to apply the two huge waste products (fly-ash and sludge) to the
degraded soils in the region, to find if this mixture promotes better growth
of trees and plants (thereby achieving land restoration), to assess if the
villagers would use the new "resource", and, would there be any support/interest
from NGO's (here comes the importance of the organisations like SEEDS and 
others), government and the private sector to carry on this work once it is 
proven. The main idea is to promote land restoration and afforestration 
simultaneously so that it becomes sustainable. 
The project "Land Restoration Through Waste Management in India", officially 
commenced in November, 1993. 
The encouraging results have started coming. Dr. Powell is now compiling the
results and preparing the final progress report. He is currently visiting 
India. He has kindly agreed to provide all the documentary support on his
research. He has already faxed me a brief report.
In the second part I would cover his findings in little detail.


