So the main goal of the Fly-Ash project was:  

1) To consume the huge reserves of Fly-Ash produced over last 20-30 years in 
some productive way so that we are not left with enough of these toxic products.
2) To find a way so that currently generating Fly-Ash is consumed immediately
rendering the  surrounding region  environmentally safe.
3) and most importantly, to improve the land quality through waste management.
As I have said before, the farming lands in India is depleted of trace elements
over repeated use, and a cost effective method was necessary to restore the 
land. The commercial fertilizer has been able to provide nitrogen and 
phosphorous requirement of the farming land, but it has not been able to 
restore the land of some of its essential trace elements.
(Trace elements are: phosphorous, potassium, magnesium, calcium, zinc, 
manganese, copper, iron, boron etc.)

The Fly-Ash project was conceptualised to show how by applying certain very 
cost effective techniques all the above goals are achieved. 

How the mixture was generated?

Fly-ash was collected and trucked from Talcher to Cuttack (90 km) where it was
mixed with sludge (human waste product) and bagged (approx. 25 kg each) for
transport back to Kurunti(60 km.). The costs were as follows:

- collection/bagging/loading of 500 bags of Fly-ash at Talcher......Rs.   4/bag
- transportation of 500 bags of ash from Talcher to Cuttack.........Rs.  10/bag
- collection/bagging/transportation of 400 bags of sludge...........Rs.  15/bag
- mixing of ash + sludge at cuttack.................................Rs.   7/bag
- handling/transport of 500 bags of mixtures to Kurunti.............Rs.  14/bag
- handling of mixtures at Kurunti...................................Rs.   4/bag
							-----------------------
					total cost/25 Kg bag........Rs. 54/bag

The above calculation does not take into account the number of bags of mixture
that were not used at Kurunti (wastage), which would bring the cost per bag
applied to the field below Rs. 40 per 25 Kg.
Note: A bag of commercial fertilizer in the market costs approx. Rs. 300 per
50 Kg. bag, making the cost of the mixtures approx. 1/3 that of commercial
fertilizers. Furthermore, the cost of the mixtures is calculated as delivered
and applied to the field, which is not included in the cost of the fertilizer.

Evaluation of improved soil condition through addition of mixtures:

Detailed data on soil improvement are not included here. The soil taken by them-
selves, test at below average or sub standard levels for the various soil 
parameters (e.g., pH, organic matter, nitrogen, above trace elements, cation
exchange capacity, and base saturation). However all of these parameters are
improved dramatically when mixtures are applied. For example, organic matter is
 = 1% in the soil only (below acceptable limit) but increases to > 5%(range of
a loam soil) when mixtures are applied, and pH goes from = 5.5 (which would
require lime) to 6.5-7.0. Most of the above are not improved by adding standard
fertilizers to the soil nor will commercial fertilizers replace the much needed
bioessential elements which are removed during erosion.

Evaluation of the best species of trees for plantation:

All trees were selected with advise from SWEDFOREST and the Dpt. of Social 
Forestry. Their suggestions were based on long term reforestry trials and the
needs of local people w.r.t. to building materials, cash crop etc. Species 
include Eucalyptus, Chakhunda, Acacia, Giringa, Debadaru, Bamboo and Sugarcane.
Not all species are reacting the same to the mixtures. In general, trees 
planted in the mixtures are growing at 2-4 times the rate of trees planted in 
soil of India.

Direct Benefits:
1) Rural communities will be employed to manage plantations from which they can
harvest trees. In this aspect, they will receive payment towards using a more
sustainable type of agriculture through which they can increase their yield of
materials for construction and/or cash crop. They will also learn the benefits
of using "waste material in forestry - a type of eco-education which can be 
passed down to future populations.

2) Government and the private sector will employ people to manage large scale
plantations for either reforestration (government) or commercial purposes
(private sector).

3) There will be an economic influx for the infrastructure (transportation etc.)
to support the plantations.

