Dear friends,
I will not have enough time to elaborate in details about the soil contamination
problem that was raised before in this forum with regard to large scale use
of flyash in our agriculture land. I did have a lengthy discussion with Dr.
Mike Powell who returned from India recently. The briefs of the discussion
follow:

1) The issue of contamination is real and should be sincerely addressed. But
   according to him if the soil is properly analysed before being applied with
   flyash, this factor could be eliminated safely. Key issue is ofcourse 
   analyzing the soil.

2) He is of the view that a clear distinction be made between "Bioessential 
   level" and "toxic level". As an example Iodine and Sulphur both are necessary
   for human survival. But more dose of it might be fatal. So it would be 
   wise to find out how much flyash in a particular land be applied so that 
   the "bioessential level" of the trace heavy metals is maintained.

3) His findings on the 6 specis that he used for afforestration is encouraging.
   For measuring the contamination level, he used leaves, stems, root and trunk.
   He found that in no case did the level was above bioessential level. Also
   the examination of soil in the treated area revealed the same.

4) However he said that it would not be wise to recommend their use in 
   paddy fields (where he is sure, application would dramatically increase the
   yield). Before this is done it should be determined how it might affect
   plant and soil chemistry through research.

5) The above is his next research project if the Canadian Government agrees to
   provide funds. If this happens there is every possibility that we might
   have a lab at IIT Kharagpur for testing soil, primarily for this purpose.

6) He argues that in many parts of India the people have already started using
   flyash for high yield without even knowing its overall impact. This drives
   home idea that there should be research to find out if it would really
   contaminate the edible plant if flyash is used indiscriminately.

I am trying to get a copy of his final report. If I could then I would 
elaborate on his findings.

